    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short: **no, you should not make anything of the fact that the panel was entirely female, nor is it a sign of unfair bias.**\n\nHere is a breakdown of why, along with context on how academic hiring decisions work and what you should do next:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel's Gender Composition Is Not a Factor\n* **Both you and the successful candidate are women:** Because the hire was also a woman, there is no prima facie case for gender discrimination. \n* **Administrative and committee assignments:** In public universities, search committees are formed based on administrative roles (e.g., Head of Department, Director of Teaching, program lead), availability, and service quotas. In fields seeking to increase female representation, senior female faculty are often disproportionately called upon to serve on panels. An all-female panel is usually a coincidence of scheduling, role requirements, or committee rotation, rather than a coordinated stance.\n* **Raising it would harm your professional standing:** Bringing up the gender composition of the panel to the department or HR would appear unfounded and adversarial, and it would likely close the door to future opportunities with that department or panel members.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why the Other Candidate May Have Been Selected\nIt is understandable to feel frustrated when comparing CV metrics on paper (completed PhD vs. finishing, 6 papers vs. 2, 4 years teaching). However, in academic hiring—particularly for **lecturer** positions—committees evaluate more than publication count once candidates reach the final interview stage:\n\n* **Departmental strategic needs:** Many economics and business programs face pressure to improve student employability, meet professional accreditation standards, or teach applied policy/finance courses. The announcement specifically highlighted her *\"strong experience from industry\"*—this practical expertise may fill a specific curriculum or strategic gap the department urgently needed.\n* **Job talk and interview performance:** Once a shortlist is established, baseline qualifications are considered met. Final decisions often come down to the teaching demonstration, interpersonal fit with faculty, and how well each candidate answered specific situational questions during the two interviews.\n* **Perceived trajectory and adaptability:** Committees sometimes weigh potential and fresh industry ties as a complement to existing departmental strengths, especially if the department already has strong traditional academic coverage.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Regarding the Delay in Feedback\nTwo weeks without a response is common in university HR environments:\n* **HR sign-off:** Search committees are often barred from sending individual feedback until HR has fully closed the recruitment file, finalized the contract with the chosen candidate, and approved the feedback notes.\n* **Standard bureaucratic lag:** Academic committees often take 3–6 weeks to coordinate and issue formal written feedback.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite, professional follow-up:**  \n   If you contacted the panel chair directly, wait another week (three weeks total), then send a brief, courteous follow-up to the HR contact or search chair reiterating that you are seeking constructive feedback to aid your future applications.\n2. **Focus inquiries entirely on professional development:**  \n   Frame your request around how you can strengthen future applications (e.g., *\"Are there specific areas in my teaching demonstration or interview presentation where I could improve?\"*). \n3. **Keep the focus forward:**  \n   Reaching the final shortlist of three for a public university lecturer position demonstrates that your profile is highly competitive. Variations in departmental \"fit\" and niche curriculum needs are often the deciding factors at the final hurdle.\n",
